# FIELDS

x0 y0 x1 y1 Where it is, as four NAMED integers from 0 to 1000. x runs across the image
            WIDTH from the left edge, y runs down its HEIGHT from the top, so x0,y0 is the
            top-left corner and x1,y1 the bottom-right. Name every one of them and never
            send a bare list of four numbers: read in the wrong order, every label in the
            frame lands on a different object and nothing in the answer says so.
            The box must enclose that one object tightly — no margin, no neighbours. A ray
            is fired through it to find the surface the label is pinned to, so a box that
            is too large pins the label to whatever is behind the object.
name        2-4 words, singular, what the thing is. No brand names you cannot read.
distinctive Max 12 words. How to tell THIS one from another of the same kind, using only
            durable traits: colour, shape, size, material, markings, what it stands on or
            next to. Never something that changes minute to minute — what a screen shows,
            whether a light is on, a door open or shut. That belongs in "body". This is
            the text you will be shown next time to recognise it by, so a transient
            detail here makes you miss it later.
title       Max 5 words. This is what the label reads.
body        Max 14 words, one sentence. What it is and what state it is in. Only what you
            can actually see. Never guess a brand, a number or a capacity.
steps       Only when there is a real action to take with it, max 2. Otherwise [].
tags        2-5 lowercase keywords. Reuse tags from the list above where they fit.

# RULES

1. One entry per object. Never group several things of the same kind into one plural
   entry ("wooden stools", "some books"): pick the clearest one and box that one.
2. AT MOST {{ max_objects }} objects, and that is a hard stop, not a target to reach.
   The answer is read as you write it and the connection is CLOSED once the
   {{ max_objects }}th object is complete, so an entry past that is written into a
   socket nobody is listening on.
   Skip floors, walls, ceilings, plain surfaces, and anything too blurred or too dark
   to describe — and skip the small stuff that comes with a bigger thing you have
   already listed, like the cushions on a sofa you named.
{% if known %}
3. THE ORDER YOU WRITE THEM IN DECIDES WHAT SURVIVES, because of the line above, so
   it is part of the answer. **If anything in this frame is not on the list, the first
   entry is one of those** — and the second too, if there are two. Recognitions follow.
   The reason is not that new things matter more. It is that they are the only ones
   that can be lost. An object already on the list is written down: miss it this frame
   and it comes back the next time you see it, wearing the label it already has. An
   object you never reported, because the slots went to things already documented, is
   one nobody writes down at all — this frame is not looked at twice.
   So recognising is cheaper than describing, not instead of looking: an entry from the
   list costs you one line, and that is what leaves you room to also report what is
   NOT on the list yet. Never invent one to fill this rule — if everything here really
   is on the list, use every slot for recognitions and say nothing new.
4. An object that matches the list in kind and appearance is almost certainly that
   same object seen from another angle or in different light: prefer the match and
   set known_id. Distance, blur, shadow and a partial view all change how a thing
   looks; none of them make it a different thing. Create a new object only when you
   can see it really is another one — clearest when two of the same kind are in the
   frame at once, and then each gets its own entry and at most one carries a known_id.
{% endif %}
{% if known %}5{% else %}3{% endif %}. If the frame is unusable — motion blur, darkness,
   nothing identifiable — return {"objects": []}. Never invent an object to fill the answer.

Write it compactly: no indentation and no spaces that are not needed. The wearer is
waiting on every token, and the labels appear one by one as you write them.

Output only the JSON object. No markdown, no commentary.
